{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (proximity to truth) as the justification for preferring better-corroborated theories, but this smuggles in induction because Popper's own formal definition of verisimilitude was refuted by Tichý and Miller, leaving no non-inductive logical basis to claim one theory is 'closer to truth' than another based solely on past survival.","premise":"Popper attempted to ground the preference for corroborated theories in verisimilitude, but the Tichý-Miller theorem demonstrated that within Popper's own logical framework, no false theory can be closer to the truth than any other, collapsing the distinction required for the target's argument.","premises_essential":["d406783406de2527f4d946521167c9a039422148c650d2c6540e64b037c3893c"],"successor_question":"Given the collapse of Popper's quantitative verisimilitude, can a purely pragmatic 'critical methodological decision' be defended without implicitly assuming that past survival indicates future reliability?","target":"d406783406de2527f4d946521167c9a039422148c650d2c6540e64b037c3893c"}]}